@@ -0,0 +1,37 @@
+---
+tier: quick
+created: 2026-09-20
+status: in-progress
+owner: node
+branch: HEAD
+touches: [src/flask/app.py, tests/test_basic.py]
+---
+
+# server-name-ipv6
+
+## Why
+`Flask.run` splits bracketed IPv6 `SERVER_NAME` values at their first colon,
+so it cannot derive the configured host and port.
+
+## What
+- Parse bracketed IPv6 literals separately when deriving `run` defaults.
+- Preserve hostname parsing and configured port zero behavior.
+
+## Acceptance
+- [x] `SERVER_NAME="[::1]:8080"` makes `run()` pass `::1` and `8080` to the
+  development server — check: targeted regression test.
+- [x] `SERVER_NAME="[::1]:0"` passes port `0` — check: targeted regression
+  test.
+
+## Impact
+`Flask.run` is the affected public entry point. The Flask CLI has independent
+host and port options and does not use this fallback parsing path. No request
+routing behavior changes.
+
+## Root cause
+### Root cause: implicit-assumption
+`Flask.run` assumed a `SERVER_NAME` contains at most one colon. Bracketed IPv6
+literals contain colons before their optional port delimiter.
+
+Fix: split bracketed literals at `]`, then remove the optional port separator.
+Prevention: regression coverage for IPv6 with a configured port and port zero.
